  }))), "Background"), mdx("p", null, "As stated in the goals above, this project aims to make a neural network electrocorticography (ECoG) signal decoder that classifies vowels. ECoG signals are obtained by implanting hundreds of small electrodes deep within the brain to record electrical activity. This methodology has the potential to be useful in a variety of practical applications, particularly as a brain-computer interface. One area of interest is in restoring speech communication by decoding ECoG signals and using the decoded features to reconstruct an acoustic signal. Artificial neural networks are the current standard for ECoG decoding."), mdx("p", null, "The gold standard of speech restoration is real-time decoding and reconstruction so that the one can use their speech neuroprosthesis to communicate in a naturalistic way. The design of models and algorithms that can do this is made more difficult by the fact that little is known abou the representation of speech primitives in the brain. How does the brain use motor programs to encode discrete linguistic information into a continuous acoustic signal?"), mdx("p", null, "It has been established that ECoG signals can provide practical benefits when used as inputs to brain-computer interfaces. However, I also believe that these signals may be useful for scientific investigation into spoken communication. More specifically, I believe they may provide information about how phonetic segments are represented in the brain."), mdx("p", null, "MAY ADD SHORT PARAGRAPH ABOUT PHONETIC SEGMENT REPRESENTATIONS (PHON. FEATURES, MOTOR PROGRAMS, RDP) JUSTIFYING METHOD OF ANALYSIS"), mdx("h4", {
